the next speaker where is he is john  what's your what's your current title  john what's your job title  energy projects manager for the greater  southeast energy hub got it right  uh and john uh has previously worked  with suffolk climate change partnership  yeah anybody else i should mention  is that good enough so john  from my perspective john is the go-to  energy man  particularly on renewables so i follow  him on twitter  and every day  he he his amazing amount of information  coming from john so he really knows his  stuff absolutely so you know he's the  man i would turn to i did yesterday and  he gave me some factoids for today so  much appreciated john so john  over to you thank you very much  all right thank you very much ian um  yeah thank you for inviting me to speak  here this morning um it's great to be  back in in suffolk  speaking um to you today  i live in suffolk still i live just  north of ipswich and yeah i did spend  nearly 10 years working with babe mid  suffolk through the suffolk climate  change partnership as a community  advisor so a few familiar faces in the  room and um yeah work on community  buildings and parishes um  over that time period i now work for a  team called the greater south east  energy hub it's actually quite similar  job just  broader geography we're funded by the  government to work with local  authorities and communities to  accelerate the delivery of local energy  projects um  of all shapes and sizes and i'll be  talking specifically about the support  we offer for communities through that  um i was just thinking  um when phil had his slides up about the  net zero plans through to 2050 how  much time is that really and i was  thinking back well how 2050 is 30 years  in the future what was 30 years ago and  it's well there's a big event i can  remember from back then 1990 was that  penalty shootout in the world cup final  so actually now yeah gaza crying and all  that that's further away in time than  2050 so just to kind of put it in some  perspective that's  how much time we've now got it's time to  get on and start planning  okay  so i thought i'd just start off just by  showing this um  demonstration here i've pulled out some  data about where are we using fossil  fuels at the moment in weber and  mid-suffolk and i've got the data here  for baber  so you can see  obviously being a rural area people need  to drive transport fuel makes quite a  big chunk of our energy use still  so yeah petrol is actually  you can see the charts there the blue  line on that top graph is 2005 and the  orange line is 2018  so you can actually see petrol use has  actually risen slightly over that time  period in baber  gas use drops slightly electricity drops  slightly as well as heating oil but  those are the four prominent uses and  fossil fuels there you'll notice coals  on there we don't actually use very much  coal in the uk anymore a few barbecues  but not like we used to with power  stations and everything so that's been  the big progress over that time period  um we've made some really big changes in  the electricity sector getting coal off  the system through renewables and gas  and you can see their carbon emissions  has dropped about 25 percent since 2005  to 2018  and life doesn't feel too much different  um in terms of  back then to now so we can make some  pretty hefty  um cuts in our carbon emissions without  too much difficulty  but yeah that achievement in the carbon  reduction has primarily come through the  energy electricity sector  um  and in terms of local energy um  previously yeah getting coal off that  was the domain of people who ran power  stations the offshore wind farms it was  quite a discreet group of people that  made that change happen  whereas the next big phase of  decarbonisation that  has to evolve as a council of families  said earlier everyone in this room is  what  we use to heat our homes  and how we drive it's going to get quite  personal so whereas  the first phase of decarbonisation was a  small group of people the next phase has  to involve everybody  so as community leaders um yeah we need  to start engaging our neighbours and our  communities and start planning how we're  going to shift these  chunks of energy away from petrol and  oil and gas  onto clean energy like electricity  so phil set up it really nicely um  community owned renewable energy is what  we'll be talking about now  the government's plans that were  announced this week really set in stone  the kind of overall strategy for the  country  which is really to generate as much  low-carbon electricity as we possibly  can through renewables and nuclear and  other sources  um to then use that power to then  decarbonize other sectors like heating  and transport so the first thing to do  is see what opportunities we have to  generate electricity locally  this picture here some of you may  recognize it this is actually nail and  school  here in baber  and this was in fact the uk's first  community-owned um solar energy project  that's quite an accolade i think um so  back in 2011 when the feeding tariff was  first introduced group of parents that  were associated with the school  recognized it had a great rooftop for  solar energy  so they clubbed together they formed a  village energy cooperative they  approached the school and the county  council say well can we fund a solar  array on the school and give the  electricity for free  to the school and that's how it happened  a group of about 20 households in  neyland  um all co-invested in this solar energy  ray and got it installed on the school  and in fact that instigated the council  once they saw the opportunity with solar  energy they then did their own internal  program which saw another 90 schools in  the county  follow through with solar installations  that was instigated i would say by this  one in neyland so it shows the power of  community lead showing the initiative  and have the ripple effect it can have  on other parties  and i think that's also got a biomass  foil of that school as well  so at the greater south east energy hub  we run something called the rural  community energy fund  which is a government-funded grant  scheme which is there for parish  councils town councils and community  energy groups  um to get some extra resources  um to engage like a lot of them are  probably going to be volunteers doing it  in their spare time and some of these  projects can be quite complex  so our grant fund enables you to draw in  professional consultancy support  we offer initially a stage one  feasibility grants so you can engage  professional energy consultants  solicitors and all that  who will do the kind of technical  analysis of what renewables are suitable  for your parish or your location  they'll advise you on  the sort of appropriate legal structure  to set up like a community benefit  society or a community interest company  and they'll do the work in working out  can what's the grid connections like  what sort of planning permission do you  need to proceed  that can then be followed up with a  stage two development grant where we can  provide further funding to help you do  more detailed studies  like if you're doing a solar farm you  might need to look at the ecology or the  flooding situation that sort of thing  um but yeah we're there to get you to  the point where you can start to finance  that project through a community share  offer or getting some ethical investment  for a project  so a few case studies of how we've used  that funding to date we've now allocated  over a million pounds um through that  fund across the whole southeast of  england  and this is one project i really like  which is a solar farm in berkshire  i don't know if you're familiar with the  term a fuel allotment charity um it's  something that exists in a few  parishes  and they're actually quite historic the  fuel allotment charity was um  they were set up at the time of  enclosures  so  um when  common land was enclosed a bit of land  was often re kept back for the parish to  use they literally use it as a fuel  allotment they would grow trees on it  and other crops which they would harvest  every year and they would distribute  that wood fuel to the poor of the  village  and this is a fuel allotment charity in  berkshire still owned that land from  several hundred years ago and they'd  used it for other purposes but still  with that charitable mission to bring in  income to  offer a local benefit  but we've actually given them some grant  money to see can they use that land now  to put a solar farm up  so rather than growing trees they're now  going to use that sunlight to generate  renewable electricity sell it to the  grid sell it to local customers  and they're going to use the profits  they make from that renewable energy to  then yeah keep their original charitable  purpose going they'll use it to support  the fuel poor of their village  so they've got 20 acres of land they  don't need all of that they're going to  use about two hectares of it for a solar  farm so still plenty of space for  greenery and wildlife  yeah so  at the moment they've got stage one  funding  and yeah we're currently working with a  few organizations to see if they can get  some bridge finance to bring  construction and talk to people about  getting a power purchase agreement to  buy the electricity  um yeah the comment was made earlier  about  yeah if we're going to see a lot of  these  that community benefit that phil talked  about  yeah the government's plan is for to  have carbon free electricity in the uk  by 2035 so that is going to mean a lot  more renewable energy  um a lot of wind power but also more  solar power  so there's plenty of opportunity still  for more power on rooftops but we will  see more solar farms come forward in  fields and our landscapes  so i think it's really crucial for  communities to be involved in that  process and actually to share ownership  of these infrastructure projects that  they'll be living with and you can  actually see once you do have that  community involvement and ownership  all the profits that come from that  solar farm can be retained locally to  subsidise other local services  so  some  some communities chose to use their  surplus to support food banks during the  covered crisis  others are looking to fund rural bus  services and electric buses  um and it's also just a good way for  people to invest their savings and  pensions um to see it being good puts  good use for the next generation  so yeah community solar farms is  something we're keen to support going  forward  um the other c um  area we've been supporting is renewable  heating  so this is a project you may have heard  of it's got quite a bit of press  they're building it at the moment and  villager swath and prior just over the  border in cambridge here near new  markets  they've actually a village quite common  at  baber amid suffer ones they're off the  gas grid they're on heating oil and lpg  and electric  and so the parish council there and  they'd done a project where they'd got a  community land trust to build some  social housing  and then they thought well can we use  this kind of community building approach  in other areas and they looked at this  question how can we get the whole  village off heating oil  so that again they use the rural  community energy fund to do a study of  how they could  set up a village heating network  um so you can see a map of it there and  there's a field just on the edge of the  village where they're going to build an  energy center which will incorporate  boreholes for ground source heat pumps  there'll be an air source heat pump  there and a solar farm connecting into  it to provide power and heat  to supplement and the costs  and then  they're gonna they're currently building  um a heat main for the village so from  that energy center  insulated heating pipes will run down  the street and each house will then be  able to connect into it  using some kind of interface unit so  they'll get rid of their oil tanks  they'll get rid of their boilers  and they'll be able to interface with  this  communal heat supply um and it's been  designed to be a very um similar cost to  what people were paying for their oil  anyway  um and i think yeah if you've had to  order oil recently you can it's gone up  the same as gas has so  um doing it this way you're insulating  your community from those kind of  volatile  price hikes as well  um another version of that renewable  heating  we've also seen projects that are  looking to supply heat to a single large  energy user  so this is a project we supported in the  thames valley  just west of london so maidenhead energy  group  you can see the river thames is just  running through the picture at the back  there this is a leisure center that's  just next to the river  and we gave them some funding to see  could that use the river thames as a  heat source to supply heat via heat pump  to the leisure center they've ended up  actually going through a ground source  heat pump because i think there are  roads and other things in the way  but similar premise the community group  will own and operate the heat pump and  will have a heat supply contract with  the leisure center so  the leisure centre hasn't had to do  any of the work themselves or find the  money to build it so they're partnered  with the community energy group  they're going to raise their finance  like naylan did with people from the  area i think they've just raised about  150 200 000 pounds to build towns  and again yes an example of how people  can club together to solve a energy  issue  um for a community facility like this  um and  another variation of renewable heating  um  yeah so if we are going to be talking  about phasing gas boilers out by 2035  they're actually also talking about  phasing fossil fuel heating and off gas  grid areas sooner  because oil and lpg are more carbon  intensive fuels so there was a  consultation launch this week where  they're going to look at phasing out oil  and gas oil and lpg by actually by 2026  for new sales  so  helping rural communities understand  their options for domestic heating is  going to be really important over the  next few years and certainly recommend  using some of the grant funds that are  coming out like there and there's going  to be a home upgrade scheme a home  upgrade grant which will be available  for fuel poor households in off gas grid  areas to get insulation and heating  systems some other people in our team  will be managing that  but there could also be community  approaches to individual heat pumps as  well  so we've worked with a few villages to i  know in suffolk's running a solar  together bulk by scheme at the moment so  could a similar proposed premise operate  for domestic heat pumps  um  a few years ago when i was working in  suffolk we ran a trial project with  cookley and walpole parish council where  we worked with a few  heat pump and biomass boiler installers  say if we can guarantee you 10 15  installs in this village this heating  season  um can you offer us a discount  and through that kind of  yeah community scale  organization  um  the  installers offered a 15 discount off  their normal installation prices as if  it had been a one-off  um so yeah we're very keen to see if  there's other ways of  yeah pooling that community level um  support and education to help  rural householders make this transition  from what they're used to to other forms  of heat pumps  um i think that's the main  thing as my contact details and there if  you want to get in touch i'll be around  for the panel session earlier  um but yeah i just really want to  emphasize that point that yeah this next  phase really has to be well every parish  is going to have to have their own plan  and action so hopefully see a wave of  action sweep up the river star and up  the a140 and we're here to help  thank you  you

